Package: spax
Type: Package
Title: Spatial Accessibility and Allocation Modeling
Version: 0.7.0.9000
Authors@R:
    person("Songyos", "Rajborirug", , "songyos.r@psu.ac.th", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-5000-7409"))
Description: A modular engine for spatial access and allocation models. Methods
    of the two-step floating catchment area (2SFCA) family and an experimental
    static conditional logit allocation model with an outside option share the
    same demand, supply and travel-time inputs, decay kernels and numeric
    engine, which reads rasters in blocks and works on compact matrices. Inputs
    are areas or points with a travel-time matrix, or a population raster with
    one travel-time layer per facility. The 2SFCA-family methods give
    accessibility scores and include two-step (2SFCA; Luo and Wang (2003)
    <doi:10.1068/b29120>), enhanced (E2SFCA; Luo and Qi
    (2009) <doi:10.1016/j.healthplace.2009.06.002>), modified (M2SFCA;
    Delamater (2013) <doi:10.1016/j.healthplace.2013.07.012>), three-step
    (3SFCA; Wan, Zou and Sternberg (2012) <doi:10.1080/13658816.2011.624987>)
    and Huff-weighted variants, also for several demand groups that compete for
    the same facilities, such as age bands or transport modes (Tao et al. (2018)
    <doi:10.1186/s12913-018-3132-8>). The allocation model includes the Huff
    model (Huff (1963) <doi:10.2307/3144521>), specified with spec_huff(),
    and extends it with an outside option for demand that goes elsewhere. It
    can be evaluated at given parameters, fitted to observed facility counts or
    origin-facility flows, compared with simpler allocations and across decay
    kernels, used to predict for new locations and supply scenarios, and given
    facility-case bootstrap intervals; group-specific parameters are fitted
    only to counts recorded by group. Decay kernels, fitting losses, weights and
    spatially varying catchments can be supplied by the user.
